\[ N (a_1, b_1, c_1, a_2, b_2, c_2, s) = N (s), \]
we define for $\left. i, j \epsilon \{ \pm 1, \pm 2 \right\}$,
\[ N_{(i, j)} (s) = \{ \begin{array}{cc}
     N (a_i, b_i, c_i, a_j, b_j, c_j, s) & \tmop{if} i, j > 0\\
     N (c_i, b_i, a_i, a_j, b_j, c_j, s) & \tmop{if} i < 0, j > 0\\
     N (a_i, b_i, c_i, c_j, b_j, a_j, s) & \tmop{if} i > 0, j < 0\\
     N (c_i, b_i, a_i, c_j, b_j, a_j, s) & \tmop{if} i, j < 0
   \end{array} \begin{array}{c}
     
   \end{array} \]
Also, writing for \ $\left. i \epsilon \{ 1, 2 \right\}$
\[ T (a_i, b_i, c_i) = T_i, \]
we define
\[ T_{- i} = T (c_i, b_i, a_i) . \]
Finally, we define the matrices $R_{- i}$and $S_{- i}$ in a similar fashion.



