@@ -0,0 +1,175 @@
+# Polynomial infinitesimal automorphisms (arXiv:0709.1499)
+
+> Status: **locally verified, awaiting oracle evidence**. Every local G1–G9(c)
+> gate passes. The mandatory bare and G9 oracle runs were attempted, but the
+> OpenRouter key returned HTTP 403 “total limit exceeded” on every redraw, so the
+> harness correctly produced no hardness verdict. Do not emit this family until a
+> complete bare run records `hardened`.
+
+| Profile field | Value |
+|---|---|
+| Track | B — no-tool compression |
+| Native domain | algebra |
+| Object regime | rational exact |
+| Computational core | polynomial identity |
+| Certificate form | matrix certificate |
+| Intended intuition | change of variables: reason over abstract `a,b,c`, then substitute |
+| Domain essentiality | native |
+| Reduction | none |
+
+## What the family is
+
+The source is Norbert Riedel, [*Markoff Equation and Nilpotent
+Matrices*](https://arxiv.org/abs/0709.1499), specifically Section 2 of the last
+substantive revision, v6. It defines
+
+`M(a,b,c) = [[1,a,b],[0,1,c],[0,0,1]]`
+
+over an arbitrary commutative ring and displays its infinitesimal automorphism
+`R`, characterized up to scale by `R^T M + M R = 0`. An instance substitutes
+three sparse polynomials in `Z[x]` for `a,b,c`; the solver must return the
+normalized `3 x 3` polynomial matrix `R(x)`. The central entry is fixed to
+`c(x)^2-a(x)^2`, removing scalar ambiguity. Verification performs exact sparse
+polynomial and matrix arithmetic, never reads the planted answer, and accepts
+any witness in the stated bounded language.
+
+Generation is composition of identities: it samples the three substitutions and
+substitutes them into the paper’s universal matrix identity. It does not solve the
+resulting coefficient system. The paper’s headline Markoff-unicity theorem is not
+used: arXiv withdrew v7, and the general conjecture remains open.
+
+## Why this is Track B
+
+Track A would be false. Section 2 both states uniqueness up to a scalar and writes
+the answer explicitly; independently, the answer is obtainable by polynomial-time
+linear algebra. The implemented reference method evaluates at `D+1` finite-field
+points, solves a normalized nine-variable linear system at each point, and uses
+Newton interpolation. Its complexity is `O(D*9^3 + 9D^2)` field operations for
+`D=3n`. At shipping `n=100`, seed 800, it used 2,382,636 counted operations and
+0.165 seconds; all eight audit instances were recovered exactly.
+
+The compact route is to retain `a,b,c` as abstract ring elements, derive the
+fixed `3 x 3` identity, and expand only after substitution. It used 138 counted
+sparse coefficient operations at shipping. This is the intended compression:
+the generic route is easy for software but not executable by hand in the no-tool
+context. Section 2’s commutative-ring statement licenses the polynomial objects;
+no discrete surrogate is involved.
+
+## Worked demo
+
+The complete demo instance is:
+
+```text
+Work in Z[x]. Let a(x)=1, b(x)=0, c(x)=0 and
+M(x)=[[1,a(x),b(x)],[0,1,c(x)],[0,0,1]].
+Find a 3 by 3 integer-polynomial matrix R with
+transpose(R)M+MR=0 and R[1][1]=c(x)^2-a(x)^2.
+Degree D=0, total support K=4, coefficient height H=2.
+```
+
+Its required external answer is:
+
+```json
+<answer>[[[["1/1",[0]]],[["2/1",[0]]],[]],[[["-2/1",[0]]],[["-1/1",[0]]],[]],[[],[],[]]]</answer>
+```
+
+Equivalently, `R=[[1,2,0],[-2,-1,0],[0,0,0]]`. A person can solve this
+smallest setting by hand. The checker records:
+
+```python
+>>> verify(inst, inst["answer"])
+(True, "ok")
+>>> verify(inst, answer_with_the_2_removed)
+(False, "wrong support size: expected 4 nonzero terms")
+```
+
+## Difficulty presets
+
+| Preset | `n` | Output degree `D` | Input terms each | Coefficient bound | Status |
+|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---|
+| demo | 0 | 0 | 1 | 2 | hand-solvable; never ships |
+| easy | 40 | 120 | 2 | 7 | first bare rung; oracle infrastructure error |
+| medium | 70 | 210 | 2 | 8 | not reached |
+| hard | 100 | 300 | 2 | 9 | provisional shipping preset; locally passes |
+
+`SHIPPING_DIFFICULTY="hard"` is provisional. Degree grows the generic
+coefficient basis while certificate support remains bounded by 82 terms, so the
+ladder grows the haystack rather than the written answer.
+
+## Local gate results
+
+| Gate | Result | Measurement |
+|---|---|---|
+| G1 | pass | 12/12 planted matrices over four presets; JSON round-trip included |
+| G2 | pass | 5/5 corruptions rejected with five distinct reasons |
+| G3 | pass | tagged JSON recovered through prose and a Markdown fence |
+| G4 | pass | 0/200,000 structure-aware guesses; shipping language about 859 bits |
+| G5 | pass | demo exact count 1/3,584; shipping sample 0/200,000; strongest failing attack 2,048 checks in 0.109 s |
+| G6 | pass | four attacks each 0/8; reference algorithm 8/8 as Track B expects |
+| G7 | pass | doubled `n=200` builds, verifies, and has a larger language |
+| G8 | pass | 640/640 composed symmetries invariant and real; 20/20 unrelated keys distinct |
+| G9(c) | pass | 1,188 chars, 195 atoms, about 297 tokens; 138 intended-route operations |
+
+Across 50 shipping seeds, the largest answer was 1,240 characters, 195 atoms,
+and approximately 310 tokens. The four failing G6 attacks were high-degree
+outlier support, low-degree greedy support, 256 exact-prior random restarts, and
+the in-context confusion of the finite automorphism `M^-1 M^T-I` with the
+infinitesimal generator.
+
+## Oracle loop
+
+| Arm / preset | Scored attempts | Infrastructure errors | Outcome |
+|---|---:|---:|---|
+| bare / easy | 0 | 4 | aborted: OpenRouter key total limit exceeded |
+| hinted / hard | 0 | 4 | aborted: same quota error |
+| placebo / hard | 0 | 4 | aborted: same quota error |
+
+No model response was scored, so there is no honest `solved/attempts` hardness
+number, no `hinted - placebo` estimate, and `hinted_verdict` remains `pending`.
+The three transcript files preserve the script-produced errors. Once quota is
+restored, rerun all three arms; if the hint helps more than the placebo, that
+would support the claim that difficulty lies in discovering the abstraction.
+
+## Use
+
+```python
+from gen_0709_1499 import DIFFICULTY, make_instance, parse_answer, render, verify
+
+inst = make_instance(seed=12345, **DIFFICULTY["hard"])
+question = render(inst)
+candidate = parse_answer(model_reply)
+ok, reason = verify(inst, candidate)
+```
+
+Rerun the mandatory bare harness from this directory:
+
+```bash
+python3 ../../scripts/harden.py gen_0709_1499.py
+```
+
+After it records `hardened`, rerun `selftest()` and the isolated G9 arms, then
+emit from the repository root:
+
+```bash
+bash scripts/emit.sh 0709.1499 20 hard
+```
+
+## Caveats
+
+- The source is withdrawn. Only the Section 2 polynomial identity is used, and
+  `verify()` independently expands it on every instance; the withdrawn Markoff
+  uniqueness claim supplies neither generation nor hardness.
+- This is strictly Track B. The reference algorithm solves every local instance
+  in a fraction of a second; no complexity-theoretic hardness is claimed.
+- G4 samples uniformly from the exact declared support/height language after
+  filling the explicitly fixed central entry. Its 0/200,000 result does not model
+  symbolic pattern recognition or correlated coefficient solving.
+- A Gröbner/module-syzygy package, a CAS simplifier that recognizes the universal
+  formula, and external computer-algebra systems were not tested. The exact
+  evaluation/elimination/interpolation implementation is the strongest standard
+  recovery route tested.
+- `canonical_key` handles term reorderings, `x -> -x`, diagonal basis sign changes,
+  and the transpose/reversal swapping `a` with `c`. It is not a canonicalizer for
+  every polynomial-ring automorphism.
+- Most importantly, the oracle evidence is incomplete because of external quota.
+  Local gates alone do not authorize shipment.
