     1	# Polynomial infinitesimal automorphisms (arXiv:0709.1499)
     2	
     3	> Status: **locally verified, awaiting oracle evidence**. Every local G1–G9(c)
     4	> gate passes. The mandatory bare and G9 oracle runs were attempted, but the
     5	> OpenRouter key returned HTTP 403 “total limit exceeded” on every redraw, so the
     6	> harness correctly produced no hardness verdict. Do not emit this family until a
     7	> complete bare run records `hardened`.
     8	
     9	| Profile field | Value |
    10	|---|---|
    11	| Track | B — no-tool compression |
    12	| Native domain | algebra |
    13	| Object regime | rational exact |
    14	| Computational core | polynomial identity |
    15	| Certificate form | matrix certificate |
    16	| Intended intuition | change of variables: reason over abstract `a,b,c`, then substitute |
    17	| Domain essentiality | native |
    18	| Reduction | none |
    19	
    20	## What the family is
    21	
    22	The source is Norbert Riedel, [*Markoff Equation and Nilpotent
    23	Matrices*](https://arxiv.org/abs/0709.1499), specifically Section 2 of the last
    24	substantive revision, v6. It defines
    25	
    26	`M(a,b,c) = [[1,a,b],[0,1,c],[0,0,1]]`
    27	
    28	over an arbitrary commutative ring and displays its infinitesimal automorphism
    29	`R`, characterized up to scale by `R^T M + M R = 0`. An instance substitutes
    30	three sparse polynomials in `Z[x]` for `a,b,c`; the solver must return the
    31	normalized `3 x 3` polynomial matrix `R(x)`. The central entry is fixed to
    32	`c(x)^2-a(x)^2`, removing scalar ambiguity. Verification performs exact sparse
    33	polynomial and matrix arithmetic, never reads the planted answer, and accepts
    34	any witness in the stated bounded language.
    35	
    36	Generation is composition of identities: it samples the three substitutions and
    37	substitutes them into the paper’s universal matrix identity. It does not solve the
    38	resulting coefficient system. The paper’s headline Markoff-unicity theorem is not
    39	used: arXiv withdrew v7, and the general conjecture remains open.
    40	
    41	## Why this is Track B
    42	
    43	Track A would be false. Section 2 both states uniqueness up to a scalar and writes
    44	the answer explicitly; independently, the answer is obtainable by polynomial-time
    45	linear algebra. The implemented reference method evaluates at `D+1` finite-field
    46	points, solves a normalized nine-variable linear system at each point, and uses
    47	Newton interpolation. Its complexity is `O(D*9^3 + 9D^2)` field operations for
    48	`D=3n`. At shipping `n=100`, seed 800, it used 2,382,636 counted operations and
    49	0.224 seconds in the final self-test (0.14–0.23 seconds across repeated runs); all
    50	eight audit instances were recovered exactly.
    51	
    52	The compact route is to retain `a,b,c` as abstract ring elements, derive the
    53	fixed `3 x 3` identity, and expand only after substitution. It used 138 counted
    54	sparse coefficient operations at shipping. This is the intended compression:
    55	the generic route is easy for software but not executable by hand in the no-tool
    56	context. Section 2’s commutative-ring statement licenses the polynomial objects;
    57	no discrete surrogate is involved.
    58	
    59	## Worked demo
    60	
    61	The complete demo instance is:
    62	
    63	```text
    64	Work in Z[x]. Let a(x)=1, b(x)=0, c(x)=0 and
    65	M(x)=[[1,a(x),b(x)],[0,1,c(x)],[0,0,1]].
    66	Find a 3 by 3 integer-polynomial matrix R with
    67	transpose(R)M+MR=0 and R[1][1]=c(x)^2-a(x)^2.
    68	Degree D=0, total support K=4, coefficient height H=2.
    69	```
    70	
    71	Its required external answer is:
    72	
    73	```json
    74	<answer>[[[["1/1",[0]]],[["2/1",[0]]],[]],[[["-2/1",[0]]],[["-1/1",[0]]],[]],[[],[],[]]]</answer>
    75	```
    76	
    77	Equivalently, `R=[[1,2,0],[-2,-1,0],[0,0,0]]`. A person can solve this
    78	smallest setting by hand. The checker records:
    79	
    80	```python
    81	>>> verify(inst, inst["answer"])
    82	(True, "ok")
    83	>>> verify(inst, answer_with_the_2_removed)
    84	(False, "wrong support size: expected 4 nonzero terms")
    85	```
    86	
    87	## Difficulty presets
    88	
    89	| Preset | `n` | Output degree `D` | Input terms each | Coefficient bound | Status |
    90	|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---|
    91	| demo | 0 | 0 | 1 | 2 | hand-solvable; never ships |
    92	| easy | 40 | 120 | 2 | 7 | first bare rung; oracle infrastructure error |
    93	| medium | 70 | 210 | 2 | 8 | not reached |
    94	| hard | 100 | 300 | 2 | 9 | provisional shipping preset; locally passes |
    95	
    96	`SHIPPING_DIFFICULTY="hard"` is provisional. Degree grows the generic
    97	coefficient basis while certificate support remains bounded by 82 terms, so the
    98	ladder grows the haystack rather than the written answer.
    99	
   100	## Local gate results
   101	
   102	| Gate | Result | Measurement |
   103	|---|---|---|
   104	| G1 | pass | 12/12 planted matrices over four presets; JSON round-trip included |
   105	| G2 | pass | 5/5 corruptions rejected with five distinct reasons |
   106	| G3 | pass | tagged JSON recovered through prose and a Markdown fence |
   107	| G4 | pass | 0/200,000 structure-aware guesses; shipping language about 859 bits |
   108	| G5 | pass | demo exact count 1/3,584; shipping sample 0/200,000; strongest failing attack 2,048 checks in 0.109 s |
   109	| G6 | pass | four attacks each 0/8; reference algorithm 8/8 as Track B expects |
   110	| G7 | pass | doubled `n=200` builds, verifies, and has a larger language |
   111	| G8 | pass | 640/640 composed symmetries invariant and real; 20/20 unrelated keys distinct |
   112	| G9(c) | pass | 1,188 chars, 195 atoms, about 297 tokens; 138 intended-route operations |
   113	
   114	Across 50 shipping seeds, the largest answer was 1,240 characters, 195 atoms,
   115	and approximately 310 tokens. The four failing G6 attacks were high-degree
   116	outlier support, low-degree greedy support, 256 exact-prior random restarts, and
   117	the in-context confusion of the finite automorphism `M^-1 M^T-I` with the
   118	infinitesimal generator.
   119	
   120	## Oracle loop
   121	
   122	| Arm / preset | Scored attempts | Infrastructure errors | Outcome |
   123	|---|---:|---:|---|
   124	| bare / easy | 0 | 4 | aborted: OpenRouter key total limit exceeded |
   125	| hinted / hard | 0 | 4 | aborted: same quota error |
   126	| placebo / hard | 0 | 4 | aborted: same quota error |
   127	
   128	No model response was scored, so there is no honest `solved/attempts` hardness
   129	number, no `hinted - placebo` estimate, and `hinted_verdict` remains `pending`.
   130	The three transcript files preserve the script-produced errors. Once quota is
   131	restored, rerun all three arms; if the hint helps more than the placebo, that
   132	would support the claim that difficulty lies in discovering the abstraction.
   133	
   134	## Use
   135	
   136	```python
   137	from gen_0709_1499 import DIFFICULTY, make_instance, parse_answer, render, verify
   138	
   139	inst = make_instance(seed=12345, **DIFFICULTY["hard"])
   140	question = render(inst)
   141	candidate = parse_answer(model_reply)
   142	ok, reason = verify(inst, candidate)
   143	```
   144	
   145	Rerun the mandatory bare harness from this directory:
   146	
   147	```bash
   148	python3 ../../scripts/harden.py gen_0709_1499.py
   149	```
   150	
   151	After it records `hardened`, rerun `selftest()` and the isolated G9 arms, then
   152	emit from the repository root:
   153	
   154	```bash
   155	bash scripts/emit.sh 0709.1499 20 hard
   156	```
   157	
   158	## Caveats
   159	
   160	- The source is withdrawn. Only the Section 2 polynomial identity is used, and
   161	  `verify()` independently expands it on every instance; the withdrawn Markoff
   162	  uniqueness claim supplies neither generation nor hardness.
   163	- This is strictly Track B. The reference algorithm solves every local instance
   164	  in a fraction of a second; no complexity-theoretic hardness is claimed.
   165	- G4 samples uniformly from the exact declared support/height language after
   166	  filling the explicitly fixed central entry. Its 0/200,000 result does not model
   167	  symbolic pattern recognition or correlated coefficient solving.
   168	- A Gröbner/module-syzygy package, a CAS simplifier that recognizes the universal
   169	  formula, and external computer-algebra systems were not tested. The exact
   170	  evaluation/elimination/interpolation implementation is the strongest standard
   171	  recovery route tested.
   172	- `canonical_key` handles term reorderings, `x -> -x`, diagonal basis sign changes,
   173	  and the transpose/reversal swapping `a` with `c`. It is not a canonicalizer for
   174	  every polynomial-ring automorphism.
   175	- Most importantly, the oracle evidence is incomplete because of external quota.
   176	  Local gates alone do not authorize shipment.

