{\tmstrong{3.10 Lemma}} If $N_{(i, j)} (s) \epsilon \tmmathbf{M}_3
(\mathbbm{Z})${\tmstrong{}} for some $\left. i, j \epsilon \{ \pm 1, \pm 2
\right\}$, then
\[ s = \frac{n}{9\mathfrak{m}} \tmop{if} i j = - 1 \tmop{or} i j = - 4 ; s =
   \frac{n}{9 g} \tmop{if} i j = 2, s = \frac{n}{9 f} \tmop{if} i j = - 2, \]
where $n$ is relatively prime to $\mathfrak{m}, g, f$, respectively.



{\tmstrong{Proof}} Consider, for instance, the case $i = 1, j = 2$. The
assumption $N_{(1, 2)} (s) = N (s) \epsilon \tmmathbf{M}_3 (\mathbbm{Z})$
implies
\[ S_2 N (s) = \frac{1}{m} (\Omega_0 + m \Omega_1) + s \Phi^t \epsilon
   \tmmathbf{M}_3 (\mathbbm{Z}), \]
hence
\[ \frac{1}{m} \Omega_0 + s \Phi^t \epsilon \tmmathbf{M}_3 (\mathbbm{Z}) . \]
Since each entry of $\Omega_0$ is divisible by 9 in case $m$ is odd, and by 18
in case $m$ is even, it follows from Corollary 3.5 and \ Lemma 3.3 a)
\[ \frac{1}{g} \Omega_0 + s \Phi^t \epsilon \tmmathbf{M}_3 (\mathbbm{Z}) \]
and this implies
\[ s \Phi^t \epsilon \frac{1}{g} \tmmathbf{M}_3 (\mathbbm{Z}) . \]
Since the greatest common divisor of all the entries of the matrix $\Phi^t$ is
9, we infer that $s \epsilon \frac{1}{9 g} \mathbbm{Z}$, as claimed. Since
Corollary 3.5 shows that the entries of $\Omega_0$ and $g$ are relatively
prime, the claim follows in this particular case. The other cases can be
proved in the same way. \ $\Box$



In light of Lemma 3.10 we introduce the following notation within the context
of Lemma 3.9:
\[ s_i = \frac{n_i}{9\mathfrak{m}} \tmop{for} i = 1, 2 ; s^+ = \frac{n^+}{9
   f}, s^- = \frac{n^-}{9 g} \]
Then Lemma 3.9 implies $n_1 = n^+ g + n^- f$, $n_2 = n^+ g - n^- f$.



{\tmstrong{Remark}} It is not difficult to show that $n_i = - \frac{2
a_i}{c_i}$ modulo $m$, $n^+ = - \frac{2 a_1}{g c_1}$ modulo 3$f$, $n^- = -
\frac{2 a_1}{f c_1}$ modulo 3$g$. But these specific representations will not
be used in the sequel.



The proof of the Theorem can now be accomplished as follows. For convenience
we shall assume that $m$ is odd. The case when $m$ is even necessitates some
minor factor 2 adjustments. \ By Proposition 1.2 and Proposition 3.1 there
exist $s^+, s^- \epsilon \mathbbm{Q}^{}$ such that $N_{(2, - 1)} (s^+), N_{(1,
2)} (s^-) \epsilon \text{ SL(3,{\tmsamp{$\mathbbm{Z})$}}}  \text{.}$ Then,
with the notation introduced above, we obtain
\[ m^2 N_{(1, 2)} (s^-) = m^2 N_{(1, 2)} (s^+ - s_2) = \]
\[ \Gamma_0 + m^2 \Gamma_2 + \frac{1}{3} (\Omega_0 + m \Omega_1) (n^+ g - n_2)
   + \frac{1}{2} ( \frac{1}{9} (n^+ g - n_2)^2 - \frac{1}{3} (n^+ g - n_2) m)
   \Phi^t = \]
\[ \Gamma_0 + m^2 \Gamma_2 - (\Omega_0 + m \Omega_1) \frac{n_2}{3} +
   \frac{1}{2} (( \frac{n_2}{3})^2 + \frac{n_2}{3} m) \Phi^t + \]
\[ \frac{1}{2} (( \frac{n^+}{3})^2 - n^+ f) g^2 \Phi^t + \frac{1}{3} (\Omega_0
   + m \Omega_1 - \frac{n_2}{3} \Phi^t) n^+ g = \]
\[ \mathfrak{A}+\mathfrak{B}+\mathfrak{C}+\mathfrak{D}, \]
where
\[ \mathfrak{A}= m^2 N_{(1, 2)} (s_2) = \Gamma_0 + m^2 \Gamma_2 + (\Omega_0 +
   m \Omega_1) \frac{n_2}{3} + \frac{1}{2} (( \frac{n_2}{3})^2 - \frac{n_2}{3}
   m) \Phi^t, \]
\[ \mathfrak{B}= \frac{1}{2} (( \frac{n^+}{3})^2 - n^+ f) g^2 \Phi^t, \]
\[ \mathfrak{C}= \frac{1}{3} (\Omega_0 + m \Omega_1 - \frac{n_2}{3} \Phi^t)
   n^+ g - 2 f g \Omega_1 n_2 +^{} n_2 f g \Phi^t, \]
\[ \mathfrak{D}= - \Omega_0 \frac{2 n_2}{3} . \]
Since, by Corollary 3.5, $a_2 = \frac{c_2}{a_1} c_1$ modulo $g^2$, and since
obviously $c_2 = \frac{c_2}{a_1} a_1$, each entry in the matrix $\mathfrak{A}$
is equal to the corresponding entry in $m^2 N_{(2, - 2)} (s_2)$ modulo $g^2$.
Since $N_{(2, - 2)} (s_2)$ is an integral matrix by Lemma 3.9, it follows that
$\mathfrak{A}$ is the null matrix modulo $g^2$. Since $\mathfrak{B}$ is also
the null matrix modulo $g^2$, and since $\mathfrak{C}$ is the null matrix
modulo $g$, we have
\[ m^2 N_{(1, 2)} (s^-) =\mathfrak{D} \tmop{modulo} g . \]
Since $n_2$ and $g$ are relatively prime, Corollary 3.5 shows that the right
hand side is not the null matrix modulo $g$. This implies that $N_{(1, 2)}
(s^-)$ is not in $\tmmathbf{M}_3$($\mathbbm{Z})$, contradicting the choice of
$s^-$ .



{\tmstrong{Remark}}{\tmstrong{}} The crux of the argument is of course the
fact that the second term in the expression $s^+ - s_2$ at the beginning of
this last sequence of manipulations has the ``wrong sign''. Since changing
that sign leads to the opposite conclusion, one can construct an alternative
proof of the Theorem, which does not rely on Proposition 1.2. First, choosing
$s_1 = \frac{n_1}{9\mathfrak{m}}$ such that $N_{(- 1, 1)} (s_1) \epsilon
\tmop{Sl} (3, \mathbbm{Z})$, one shows that there exist integers $n^+$ and
$n^-$ satisfying the identity $n_1 = n^+ g + n^- f$. Then, letting $s^+ =
\frac{n^+}{9 f}$, $s^- = \frac{n^-}{9 g}$, and following a similar line of
reasoning as above, but working with $s_1 - s^+$ and $s_1 - s^-$,
respectively, rather than with $s^+ - s_2$, one shows that both, $m^2 N_{(1,
2)} (s^-)$ and $m^2 N_{(2, - 1)} (s^+)$ are equal to the null matrix modulo
$g^2$. One can then show by related manipulations that $m^2 N_{(1, 2)} (s^-)$
and $m^2 N_{(2, - 1)} (s^+)$ are equal to the null matrix modulo $f^2$ as
well. The indicated proofs exploit the full strength of Corollary 3.5. These
two facts taken together show that $N_{(1, 2)} (s^-) \epsilon
\tmmathbf{M}_3$($\mathbbm{Z})$. Invoking finally once again the last part in
the above proof, leads to the same contradiction.





